#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 2.6.44
# DESCRIPTION
# Find the derivative of a function using the chain rule

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Chain rule (with trigonometric functions))
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('2.6')
## Problem1('44')
## KEYWORDS('Derivative', 'Chain rule')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric")->variables->add(u => 'Real');

@fn = ([ 'sin', 'cos', '+', '+' ], [ 'cos', 'sin', '-', '-' ]);

## choose a trig function and its derivative

($trig, $trigprime, $sign, $sign2) = @{ $fn[ random(0, $#fn) ] };

$a   = random(2, 5);
$p   = 2;
$x0  = random(-5, -2);
$pm1 = $p - 1;
$x1  = $a + ($x0)**$p;
$x2  = $p * ($x0)**$pm1;

$f = Formula(" $trig(u) ")->reduce;
$g = Formula(" $a + x^$p ")->reduce;

$fdisp = $f->substitute(u => $g);
$Df    = $f->D('u');
$Df    = Formula(" $Df ")->reduce;
$Dg    = $g->D('x');
$Dfg   = $Df->substitute(u => $g);
$Dfg   = Formula("$Dfg")->reduce;

$output = Formula(" $Dg*$Df ")->reduce;
$output = $output->substitute(u => $g);
$output = Formula(" $output ")->reduce;

$m    = $output->substitute(x => $x0);
$y0   = $fdisp->eval(x => $x0);
$line = Formula("$m (x - $x0) + $y0")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML

Find an equation of the line that is tangent to the graph of [`` y(x) = [$fdisp] ``] at [`` x = [$x]. ``]

Equation of the tangent line is [` y = `] [_]{$line}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

Taking [`  u(x) = [$g] `] in the generalized derivative formula for [` [$f]`] yields

 [``` \frac{d \;[$fdisp]}{dx}  = \frac{d \; [[$f]]}{dx} = [$Df] \frac{du}{dx} = [$output]. ```]

Now, when [`` x = [$x0], y = [$trig]([$x1]) ``] and [`` y'([$x0]) = [$sign] [$x2] [$trigprime]([$x1]). ``]

Hence, equation of tangent line is [`` y = [$trig]([$x1]) [$sign] [$x2] [$trigprime]([$x1])(x-[$x0]) ``]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
